          "research_question": "This systematic review synthesizes recent literature to identify how zoonotic influenza viruses move between animal populations and spill over into human communities, alongside the variables that facilitate these cross-species transfers.",
          "study_design": "The authors conducted a systematic review adhering to PRISMA standards, screening thousands of database records to select relevant publications spanning seven years.",
          "population_setting": "The analysis encompasses studies involving wild and domestic animal reservoirs, as well as human populations affected by cross-species viral transfer, without restricting geographic boundaries.",
          "main_findings": "The synthesis highlights existing knowledge on host, environmental, and viral drivers of transmission while revealing substantial unexplored areas regarding interspecies movement, ecological influences, and regional zoonotic risks.",
          "public_health_relevance": "Clarifying these transmission mechanisms supports improved pandemic preparedness and underscores the importance of coordinated, multi-sectoral health frameworks to mitigate future spillover threats.",
          "limitations": "The authors acknowledge that the reviewed corpus contains notable gaps, specifically regarding viral circulation across varied animal hosts, the precise influence of ecological variables, and regional variations in cross-species risk.",
          "gids_interpretation": "This work situates ongoing academic inquiry into viral ecology within established international research priorities, offering a consolidated reference point for scholars tracking cross-species pathogen movement.",
